{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1154 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "65dcd1502a35e09e2ca8bd2c0aea3f9804aa27f772207cbf4188880420fdebfc" :edb/db-before-t 1153 :edb/db-after-t 1154 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534466 50 #inst "2026-10-07T06:27:26.560Z" 13194139534466 true] [17592186096419 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f'(x) - 3\\sin(2x) = 0$." 13194139534466 false] [17592186096419 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f'(x) - 3\\sin(2x) = 0.$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186096419 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3\\sin{2x}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int f^{\\prime }(x)dx &= \\int 3\\sin 2xdx \\\\\nf(x) &= 3\\int \\sin 2xdx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\cos 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534466 false] [17592186096419 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3\\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186096420 1100 "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186096420 1100 "Solve the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x$." 13194139534466 false] [17592186096420 1145 "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}dx &= \\int xdx \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int (\\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + C)dx \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{6}x^{3} + Cx + D\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D$. We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not." 13194139534466 false] [17592186096420 1145 "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not." 13194139534466 true] [17592186096421 1100 "Solve the differential equation $y'  = 2x,$ and sketch the solution curves of this equation." 13194139534466 true] [17592186096421 1100 "Solve the differential equation $y' = 2x$, and sketch the solution curves of this equation." 13194139534466 false] [17592186096421 1145 "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^2+C\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C$. The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/3665168b0c99cbb50812c142637a625a.png)" 13194139534466 false] [17592186096421 1145 "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^2+C.\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/assets/e37771799ef5fadc259e6ea0073042a83e0974b5a2619d1af3e87a7467e6d780.png)" 13194139534466 true] [17592186096422 1100 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2$.\n$f'(x) - \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2} = 0$" 13194139534466 false] [17592186096422 1100 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2.$\n\n$$\nf'(x) - \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2} = 0\n$$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186096422 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int f^{\\prime }(x)dx &= \\int \\frac{3}{1 + x^{2}}dx \\\\\nf(x) &= 3\\int \\frac{1}{1 + x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 3\\arctan x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\arctan{x} + C\n$$\n\nTo find a particular solution, we substitute the condition\n\n$$\nf(0) = 2\n$$\n\ninto our solution and obtain a value for $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3\\arctan (x) + C \\\\\n2 &= 3\\arctan (0) + C \\\\\n2 &= 0 + C \\\\\nC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\arctan x + 2\n$$" 13194139534466 false] [17592186096422 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x) = 3\\arctan{x} + C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0) = 2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ 2 & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\arctan x + 2.\n$$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186096617 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289746 13194139534466 true] [17592186096617 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289753 13194139534466 true] [17592186096617 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289760 13194139534466 true] [17592186096617 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289767 13194139534466 true] [17592186096618 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289774 13194139534466 true] [17592186096618 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289781 13194139534466 true] [17592186096618 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289833 13194139534466 true] [17592186096618 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289847 13194139534466 true] [17592186096619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289809 13194139534466 true] [17592186096619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289816 13194139534466 true] [17592186096619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289826 13194139534466 true] [17592186096619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289840 13194139534466 true] [17592186096620 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289788 13194139534466 true] [17592186096620 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289795 13194139534466 true] [17592186096620 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289802 13194139534466 true] [17592186096620 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289823 13194139534466 true] [17592186096620 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289854 13194139534466 true] [17592186097219 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534466 false] [17592186097219 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534466 true] [17592186097219 1100 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{e}^{2x}=-2x.$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186097219 1100 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} + 4e^{2x} =-2x$." 13194139534466 false] [17592186097219 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{2x}-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186097219 1145 "Isolate the derivative: $y'=-2x-4e^{2x}$. Integrating term by term gives\n$$y=-x^2-2e^{2x}+C.$$\nDifferentiation recovers $-2x-4e^{2x}$." 13194139534466 false] [17592186097220 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534466 true] [17592186097220 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534466 false] [17592186097220 1100 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} =-10$." 13194139534466 false] [17592186097220 1100 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-10.$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186097220 1145 "Integrate twice, introducing a constant at each integration:\n$$y'=-10x+C,\\qquad y=-5x^2+Cx+D.$$\nThe two constants cannot be combined because one multiplies $x$." 13194139534466 false] [17592186097220 1145 "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186097223 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534466 false] [17592186097223 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534466 true] [17592186097223 1100 "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3(x+1)^{2}.$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186097223 1100 "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} =-3(x + 1)^{2}$." 13194139534466 false] [17592186097223 1145 "Integrate $y'=-3(x+1)^2$:\n$$y=-(x+1)^3+C.$$\nThese are vertical translations of a decreasing cubic with a horizontal inflection point at $x=-1$. Select the diagram whose decreasing cubic curves flatten at $x=-1$." 13194139534466 false] [17592186097223 1145 "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/assets/03a5bf5134dc7d551b2602145c4c25081a2ad274f02c95845e5789b288e618a1.png)" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289746 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289747 13194139534466 true] [17592186289746 1089 "q-119032" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289746 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534466 true] [17592186289746 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289746 1113 #uuid "ae4d3203-76af-57e6-914e-e9a022b4a7ca" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289746 1145 "First, notice that the derivative term is already isolated on the left:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289748 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289749 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289750 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289751 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289752 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1041 "selection" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289749 13194139534466 true] [17592186289747 1110 #uuid "7f4971da-77d9-5b10-9742-4958f510bb24" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289748 1034 "f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289748 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534466 true] [17592186289748 1111 #uuid "fe1ffa70-41ba-592d-9221-889fddb97a61" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289749 1034 "f(x)=-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289749 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534466 true] [17592186289749 1111 #uuid "5e89e576-486a-554f-a8e4-561e6e58ad62" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289750 1034 "f(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289750 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534466 true] [17592186289750 1111 #uuid "abe66a86-26ba-52ce-ae99-cda0b40a5423" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289751 1034 "f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289751 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534466 true] [17592186289751 1111 #uuid "d68943c3-babc-5cca-bb4a-6060331890a9" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289752 1034 "f(x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289752 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534466 true] [17592186289752 1111 #uuid "f7d5de22-29dc-571a-9a04-6e24438dc027" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289753 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289754 13194139534466 true] [17592186289753 1089 "q-119034" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289753 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534466 true] [17592186289753 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{x-1}.$" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289753 1113 #uuid "026bb192-a837-5fcc-8667-53e2260d5753" 13194139534466 true] [17592186289753 1145 "First, notice that the derivative term is already isolated on the left:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and